# T4-3: `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.github/` and a mis-anchored `alembic/versions/*`
# used to sit here, and all four were removed.
#
# NOTE, because PRODUCTION_PLAN's T4-3 got this wrong: it claimed `CLAUDE.md` and
# `AGENTS.md` were "already tracked, so the rules were dead". They were **not**
# tracked — `git log` shows neither was ever committed — so removing those two
# lines newly commits both files. That is intended (the files that govern how
# agents work in this repo belong to the repo, not to one laptop), but it is a
# real change in what gets published, not the no-op the plan described.
#
# The other two were unambiguous foot-guns: `.github/` meant a new CI workflow
# silently vanished unless force-added, and `alembic/versions/*` was root-anchored
# so it never matched `backend/alembic/versions/` — a landmine one directory move
# away from making migrations uncommittable.

# Local agent state. `settings.json` hardcodes absolute paths under one user's
# home directory, so committing it would break for anyone else; `settings.local.json`
# is local-only by convention.
.claude/

# Generated knowledge graph (`graphify update .`). Was tracked — 223 files, 18 MB,
# rewritten wholesale on every run — and untracked deliberately:
#
#   * it is derived output. Every fact in it comes from a file already in the repo,
#     so it is regenerable and adds no information a clone doesn't already have;
#   * `cache/last_query_stamp` is rewritten by every `graphify query`, so the
#     working tree was permanently dirty just from reading the graph;
#   * it kept re-breaking the gitleaks job. Baking doc prose into JSON turns
#     unquoted markdown into a quoted high-entropy value, which is exactly what
#     the `generic-api-key` rule matches. `docs/PRODUCTION_PLAN.md`'s "(API-key
#     brute force, scan/recompute abuse)" became a "leaked credential" the moment
#     it was serialised into a node label. That is an open-ended treadmill: any
#     future doc edit near the word "key" mints a new false positive.
#
# Bootstrap a fresh clone with `graphify update .` — see the graphify section of
# `CLAUDE.md`. NOTE: history still contains the old blobs, so `.gitleaks.toml`
# must keep allowlisting this path (`gitleaks detect` scans commits, not the tree).
graphify-out/